From 1f2307806f615bbb42271dd50fb6ce099471c64d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Allen Gregory <allengregoryiv@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 21:04:55 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Web UI: native cameras show their real detection rate and
 latency; the UI fps is labelled the preview

Native cameras detect and publish every frame natively, but the Java pipeline that feeds the UI runs
capped (30 fps while a stream is watched, 5 otherwise), so the dashboard read 30 fps for cameras running
at 196/120/60. CameraHealthPublisher already computes the native rate (published-count delta per ~1 s
window) and mean capture -> publish latency (NapaDetectorJNI.nativeLatencyMs) for NT health; it now
keeps them for UIDataPublisher, which sends nativeFps/nativeLatency with each UI result (only while the
camera publishes natively). The dashboard Cameras card and the Cameras page show them as the main
numbers and label the Java rate "Preview N fps (screen only; detection runs at M fps)". Non-native
cameras are unchanged. No JNI change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../src/components/cameras/CamerasView.vue    | 11 +++++++--
 .../src/components/dashboard/CamerasCard.vue  | 23 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../src/types/PhotonTrackingTypes.ts          |  4 ++++
 .../dataflow/websocket/UIDataPublisher.java   | 13 ++++++++++
 .../processes/CameraHealthPublisher.java      | 24 ++++++++++++++++++-
 .../vision/processes/VisionModule.java        |  8 +++++--
 6 files changed, 76 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

